{"id": 13, "ts": 1780798530.556119, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 1, "agent": "agent_2", "message": "I appreciate Agent\u202f3\u2019s vote for Candidate\u202fB and the strong case made for his reliability and stress handling. However, before we can endorse B, we still need to compare him against the other candidates, especially on the criteria where B shows weaknesses\u2014namely crew cooperation and attention to numeric details.\nFrom my notes, Candidate\u202fA demonstrates excellent situational awareness (\u201canticipates dangerous situations,\u201d \u201csees complex connections,\u201d \u201cexcellent spatial vision\u201d) and strong leadership, which are valuable for long\u2011distance operations. Yet A is also regarded as a \u201cshow\u2011off\u201d and \u201cnot open to new ideas,\u201d raising concerns about crew cooperation and adaptability.\nCandidate\u202fC keeps calm in a crisis, makes quick correct decisions, and understands complicated technology\u2014key for stress resilience and technical competence. But C\u2019s difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric reputation, and lack of willingness to further education suggest serious crew\u2011cooperation and adaptability issues.\nCandidate\u202fD responds adequately to unexpected events, concentrates well, solves problems extremely well, and takes responsibility seriously\u2014indicating solid stress resilience, technical competence, and responsibility. The only red flags are his \u201cmoody\u201d temperament and being seen as a \u201cloner,\u201d which could affect crew cooperation, though we have no concrete evidence of disruptive behavior.\nGiven that B\u2019s numeric memory is weak (potentially impacting attention/accuracy) and his communication style is problematic, I see D as a stronger overall alternative: D matches B on stress resilience and responsibility, and his problem\u2011solving and concentration may offset the modest crew\u2011cooperation concerns, whereas A and C have more